{"state": {"case_id": "MB_18", "timestep": 4, "case_summary": "A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain. The pain has been dull and progressive, but became suddenly worse while she was exercising. Her medical history is notable for depression, anxiety, and gonococcal urethritis that was appropriately treated. She is sexually active and does not use condoms. She admits to drinking at least 5 standard alcoholic drinks a day. The patient also recently lost a large amount of weight for a fitness show she planned on entering. The patient's current medications include oral contraceptive pills, fluoxetine, alprazolam, ibuprofen, acetaminophen, and folate. Her temperature is 99.5°F (37.5°C), blood pressure is 80/40 mmHg, pulse is 110/minute, and respirations are 15/minute with an oxygen saturation of 96% on room air. On physical exam, you note an athletic young woman with burly shoulders, a thick neck, and acne on her forehead and back. On abdominal exam you note diffuse tenderness with 10/10 pain upon palpation of the right upper quadrant. Intravenous fluids are started and labs are sent. A urinary ß-hCG has been ordered. Which of the following is the most likely the diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Ectopic implantation of a blastocyst\nB. Inflammation of the pancreas\nC. Obstruction of blood flow through the hepatic vein\nD. Obstruction of the common bile duct by radio-opaque stones\nE. Vascular ectasia within the liver\n", "root": {"label": "Acute Right Upper Quadrant Abdominal Pain with Hemodynamic Shock and Possible Obstetric or Hepatic Vascular Aetiology", "time_course": "acute", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.5, "supporting_facts": ["Sudden worsening of RUQ pain during exercise", "Hypotension (80/40 mmHg) and tachycardia (110 bpm) indicating shock", "Hemodynamically unstable state with ongoing IV fluid resuscitation", "Sexual activity without contraception requiring pregnancy exclusion", "Recent significant weight loss and chronic alcohol use as risk factors"], "excluded_candidates": ["Acute cholecystitis: less likely without jaundice or fever but remains possible", "Hepatic rupture: possible in context of weight loss and exercise but lacks trauma history", "Septic shock from intra-abdominal source: lacks localized peritoneal signs but remains competitive", "Acute mesenteric ischemia: could explain shock but atypical for patient demographics"], "alarm_features": ["Hemodynamic instability (80/40 mmHg BP with 110 bpm pulse)", "Severe RUQ pain (10/10) with diffuse tenderness", "Recent pregnancy risk exposure (sexual activity without condoms)", "Chronic alcohol use and recent significant weight loss"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Acute Obstetric Emergency", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.525096525096525, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["Lack of adnexal findings weakly disfavors obstetric emergencies, but pregnancy exclusion (via β-hCG) remains critical due to hemodynamic instability."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "urgency", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Acute Biliary Tract Disease", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.19, "posterior": 0.10479867622724766, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Severe RUQ pain with diffuse tenderness is weakly supportive of acute biliary tract disease, though the absence of jaundice or Murphy's sign would later contra…"], "evidence_against": ["Absence of fever and Murphy's sign provides meaningful reduction in support for acute biliary tract disease, though RUQ pain remains weakly 